CAT = "nzfaidScene"; SEG = "SCN"
# Scene safety, DRSABCD, calling 111, the unconscious breathing casualty, moving people, several casualties.
Q = [
("You reach a collapsed worker in a ceiling space full of exposed wiring. What comes first under DRSABCD?",
 "Check the area is safe", ["Ask loudly if they're okay", "Ring 111 for an ambulance", "Open their airway"],
 "D for Danger comes before Response, Send for help and Airway. A rescuer who is hurt cannot help anyone, so check for hazards to yourself, bystanders and the casualty first."),
("How does ANZCOR suggest you check whether a collapsed person responds?",
 "Talk and touch", ["Pinch the earlobe hard", "Splash water on the face", "Rub the breastbone"],
 "ANZCOR uses a 'talk and touch' approach: give a simple command such as 'open your eyes' and grasp and squeeze the shoulders. Painful stimuli are not part of the first aider's check."),
("A collapsed person only groans and never opens their eyes when you speak to them. How do you treat them?",
 "As unconscious", ["As fully alert", "As drowsy but safe", "As having fainted only"],
 "ANZCOR says a person who does not respond, or only groans without opening their eyes, is managed as unconscious. That means checking the airway and breathing straight away."),
("What number reaches an ambulance, fire or police in New Zealand?",
 "111", ["000", "999", "112"],
 "111 is New Zealand's emergency number; the call taker connects you to ambulance, fire or police. 000 is Australia's number and 999 the UK's."),
("When you ring 111 for a casualty, when should you end the call?",
 "When the call taker says so", ["Once you've given the address", "When the ambulance is in view", "After the first question"],
 "Hato Hone St John advises staying on the line until the call taker tells you to hang up. They may give you CPR or other instructions while help is on the way."),
("What must a deaf person do before they can text 111 in an emergency?",
 "Register for 111 TXT", ["Text 111 a test message", "Ask police for a code", "Install a 111 phone app"],
 "The 111 TXT service is for deaf, hard of hearing and speech-impaired people, and it only works once the phone has been registered. A test text to 111 is not a registration."),
("A workmate feels unwell but you are unsure they need an ambulance. Which free line gives health advice?",
 "Healthline", ["National Poisons Centre", "Police non-emergency 105", "PlunketLine"],
 "Healthline (0800 611 116) gives free advice when you are unsure what care someone needs. The Poisons Centre is for poisonings, and anything life-threatening still goes to 111."),
("An unresponsive adult is breathing normally. Where should they be placed while you wait for help?",
 "On their side", ["Sitting up in a chair", "Flat on their back", "Face down on the floor"],
 "ANZCOR recommends the side-lying recovery position for an unresponsive person who is breathing normally, so the airway stays open and fluids can drain. Lying flat on the back lets the tongue and vomit block the airway."),
("A faint office worker slumps in a chair and stops responding. What should you avoid?",
 "Head between the knees", ["Lowering them to the floor", "Rolling them onto their side", "Checking their breathing"],
 "ANZCOR says not to leave an unresponsive person sitting in a chair or push the head between the knees. Lower them gently to the ground and check breathing."),
("Two people are hurt: one is shouting with a broken arm, the other is silent and unresponsive. Who comes first?",
 "Silent unresponsive person", ["Person with the broken arm", "Whoever is closer to you", "The older of the two"],
 "ANZCOR gives priority to the unconscious person, whose airway may be blocked. A person who can shout has an open airway for now."),
("An unconscious crash victim may have a spinal injury and is struggling to breathe. Which takes priority?",
 "Airway", ["Keeping the spine still", "Removing their helmet", "Splinting the limbs"],
 "ANZCOR says the airway takes priority over any injury, including a possible spinal injury. Handle the head and neck gently, but open and clear the airway."),
("Your unresponsive casualty starts to vomit while lying on their back. What should you do?",
 "Roll them onto their side", ["Sit them up against a wall", "Raise both legs high", "Press on the stomach"],
 "Roll them promptly onto their side, open the mouth and let it drain, turning the face slightly downwards. Leaving them on the back risks inhaling the vomit."),
("At a road crash, a power line lies across a car. How far back should you stay until the lines company says it is safe?",
 "At least 10 metres", ["At least 2 metres", "At least 5 metres", "At least 50 metres"],
 "ANZCOR says not to touch the vehicle or try a rescue within 10 metres of fallen power lines until the electrical authority confirms they are safe."),
("A worker has had an electric shock and now seems completely fine. What does ANZCOR advise?",
 "See a doctor anyway", ["Go back to work", "Rest for ten minutes", "Watch them for an hour"],
 "ANZCOR says everyone who has had an electric shock needs medical assessment, because heart rhythm problems and hidden burns can follow even when the person feels well."),
("A motorcyclist is unconscious but breathing well with their helmet on. When should a first aider remove the helmet?",
 "If the airway needs managing", ["Always, as soon as you arrive", "Never, under any circumstances", "Only after they wake up"],
 "ANZCOR advises removing a helmet only if it is needed to manage the airway, help breathing or control bleeding. Otherwise leave it on and support the head."),
("You are alone and must drag an unconscious person away from a fire. Which method suits a single rescuer?",
 "Shoulder drag on the floor", ["Lift them over your back", "Carry them by their belt", "Roll them end over end"],
 "ANZCOR lists the ankle drag or arm and shoulder drag for a single rescuer, avoiding bending or twisting the neck and back. Lifts and carries risk both rescuer and casualty."),
("Why check an unconscious person's wrist and neck before treating them?",
 "For a medical alert", ["To feel for a pulse", "For signs of a fracture", "To find their ID card"],
 "ANZCOR advises looking for medical alert jewellery or devices, which can reveal conditions such as diabetes, epilepsy or allergy and guide your care."),
("Several workers want to help at a collapse. What is the best role for the most experienced first aider?",
 "Stay with the casualty", ["Go to meet the ambulance", "Direct traffic outside", "Fetch the first aid kit"],
 "ANZCOR suggests the most experienced rescuer takes charge and stays with the person while others send for help, fetch the AED and meet the ambulance."),
("What do standard precautions mean when you treat a bleeding casualty?",
 "Gloves before touching blood", ["Washing hands only afterwards", "Treating only known carriers", "Avoiding all contact with blood"],
 "Standard precautions treat all blood and body fluids as potentially infectious, so put on gloves (and eye protection if available) before contact. They apply to everyone, not just known carriers."),
("You have no gloves and must press on a bleeding wound. What can protect your hand?",
 "Plastic bag", ["Paper towel", "Thin cotton sock", "Your bare palm"],
 "Hato Hone St John suggests a plastic bag over your hand if gloves are not available. Paper and cloth soak through and do not stop contact with blood."),
("At night a person lies on a wet roadside in the cold. Besides first aid, what should you add?",
 "Shelter from the weather", ["A cold drink of water", "A brisk walk to warm up", "Their phone to ring family"],
 "ANZCOR advises protecting the casualty from the weather, for example covering them and keeping them warm, while you treat them and wait for help."),
("A casualty is found face down and unresponsive. What may you do to assess them?",
 "Roll them onto their back", ["Leave them exactly as found", "Lift them into a chair", "Sit them against a wall"],
 "ANZCOR allows rolling a face-down unresponsive person onto the back to assess and resuscitate them, handling the head and neck gently."),
("Which description fits a casualty who is unconscious?",
 "Unrousable and unaware", ["Confused but talking", "Sleepy but obeys you", "Upset and crying loudly"],
 "ANZCOR describes unconsciousness as being unrousable and unresponsive, unaware of the surroundings and with no purposeful response. A confused person who talks is still conscious."),
("While you wait with an unconscious person in the recovery position, what should you keep doing?",
 "Recheck their breathing", ["Give them sips of water", "Keep moving their limbs", "Shake them every minute"],
 "ANZCOR says to keep the airway open and recheck the person constantly, because breathing can stop. Nothing should go by mouth while they are unconscious."),
]
